  "case": {
    "presentation": "A 57-year-old female with COPD (due to 10-pack year history of smoking) presents with many seborrheic keratoses that multiplied on her back over the last few months. Is this normal?",
    "recommendations": "While seborrheic keratoses are common in midlife, a sudden eruptive onset raises the possibility of the paraneoplastic Leser-Trélat sign. This is rare but it would be important to ensure completion of age- and risk-appropriate cancer screening including basic labs and a CT chest given her smoking history. Because eruptive SKs most often associate with gastrointestinal tumors, referral for GI evaluation including endoscopy (upper and lower) is a reasonable next step. The patient should promptly report red-flag symptoms such as unintentional weight loss or GI bleeding."
  },
